@@ -0,0 +1,13 @@
+# Periodic storage maintenance
+
+The existing local 60-second maintenance caller continues to honor startup quiet mode and now yields while foreground requests are active or were active in the previous five seconds. Each tick has a 45-second work window, leaving room before the next timer event. It reuses the existing TTL handler and native state-storage operations; no new timer, provider, or queue is introduced.
+
+TTL pages run under the same durable `runId` and ordinal until the handler reports completion or the window yields. The handler owns its stable run origin and cutoff. A later tick resumes the saved run, and a completed run is not counted as physical compaction.
+
+When due, the caller drives native compaction through `compact_status` and bounded `compact_apply` batches: activate an idle migration, resume copy at the engine cursor, cut only after rows and vectors are complete, then collect until the native receipt reports `gc_complete`. Copy requests use a 128-item limit; collection uses the accepted 4,096-item limit while the native engine processes 128-item chunks, with a 4 MiB and 3-second request budget. Each tick can drain multiple requests within its 45-second window. Reclaim uses the accepted maintenance mode of up to 4,096 pages or 3 seconds per request; the ordinary reclaim path retains its 16-page, 25-millisecond limit and 5-second cooldown. The cycle respects native eligibility, backoff, lease/fence checks, and foreground pressure. Progress is retained by the native phase/cursors and the periodic state. Rollback, stale identity, unknown responses, pressure, or an incomplete reclaim stop the cycle without advancing the success baseline.
+
+The exact `compact_apply` request is stored before dispatch. An uncertain acknowledgement is retried with the same operation ID and unchanged request while the same owner/fence can still be refreshed. If the lease expires, a new exclusive lease compares `compact_status.generation`: an unchanged generation proves the request did not commit, so its exact request is archived as `not_applied` before a new ID is allocated. If generation advanced, `compact_status` does not expose an operation ID and the current contract has no receipt lookup. The caller records `compactOutcomeUnknown`, preserves the pending request and baseline, and pauses later automatic cycles; it does not infer success or issue another compaction request. This state needs receipt evidence before automatic work can resume.
+
+The first growth reference is an initial observation taken from a complete diagnostic sample after TTL cleanup has completed. It does not update `lastSuccessfulCompactionAt`. Subsequent growth maintenance is due at an additional 1,000,000,000 decimal bytes over that reference; the existing 24-hour free-page trigger remains. The reference advances only after copy/cut, collection, and reclaim all complete with no remaining free pages. Zero reclaimed bytes can still be a successful full cycle; partial reclaim cannot.
+
+Older percentage-based references are discarded when read so the first qualifying post-TTL sample initializes the new reference. The last credited full compaction timestamp and native generation remain unchanged until a complete new cycle is recorded. Manual incremental migration and opportunistic reclaim do not advance this periodic-cycle baseline.
